WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.597
A subquery in SELECT must behave like a scalar for each output row.

00:00:04.597 --> 00:00:08.347
If it is uncorrelated, the same value may repeat.

00:00:08.347 --> 00:00:12.427
If it is correlated, the value can change from row to row.

00:00:12.427 --> 00:00:18.062
This placement is useful when you want to enrich each row with one related summary value.

00:00:18.062 --> 00:00:23.287
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:23.287 --> 00:00:29.642
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:29.642 --> 00:00:32.172
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:32.172 --> 00:00:37.897
In a real setting such as a compliance workflow checking whether related evidence exists,

00:00:37.897 --> 00:00:44.157
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:44.157 --> 00:00:46.652
before discussing syntax.

00:00:46.652 --> 00:00:53.200
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
